{
 "id": "3.6",
 "module": "m53581",
 "chapter": "3",
 "title": "The Chain Rule",
 "short": "The Chain Rule",
 "lead": "The <span data-concept=\"chain-rule\">chain rule</span> differentiates a <span data-concept=\"composite-function\">composite function</span>: the <span data-concept=\"derivative\">derivative</span> of the outer function, taken at the inner one, times the derivative of the inner one. It combines with the power, trigonometric, product and quotient rules, applies again to compositions of three or more functions, and reads most simply in <span data-concept=\"leibniz-notation\">Leibniz’s notation</span>.",
 "objectives": [
  "State the chain rule for the composition of two functions.",
  "Apply the chain rule together with the power rule.",
  "Apply the chain rule and the product/quotient rules correctly in combination when both are necessary.",
  "Recognize the chain rule for a composition of three or more functions.",
  "Describe the proof of the chain rule."
 ],
 "summary_html": "<ul><li>The <span data-concept=\"chain-rule\">chain rule</span> allows us to differentiate compositions of two or more <span data-concept=\"function\">functions</span>. It states that for $\\kh(x)=\\kf(\\kg(x)),$ $$\\khprime(x)=\\kfprime(\\kg(x))\\kgprime(x).$$ In <span data-concept=\"leibniz-notation\">Leibniz’s notation</span> this rule takes the form $$\\kdydx=\\kdydu\\cdot \\kdudx.$$</li><li>We can use the <span data-concept=\"chain-rule\">chain rule</span> with other rules that we have learned, and we can derive formulas for some of them.</li><li>The <span data-concept=\"chain-rule\">chain rule</span> combines with the <span data-concept=\"power-rule\">power rule</span> to form a new rule: $$\\text{If}\\;\\kh(x)={(\\kg(x))}^{n},\\text{then}\\;\\khprime(x)=n{(\\kg(x))}^{n-1}\\kgprime(x).$$</li><li>When applied to the <span data-concept=\"composite-function\">composition of three functions</span>, the <span data-concept=\"chain-rule\">chain rule</span> can be expressed as follows: If $\\kh(x)=\\kf(\\kg(\\kk(x))),$ then $\\khprime(x)=\\kfprime(\\kg(\\kk(x))\\kgprime(\\kk(x))k^{\\prime}(x).$</li></ul>",
 "notes": "The book’s link to an animation of the chain rule is left out.",
